From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 19:43:36 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: describe the 2.4 GHz radio on the FRITZ!Box
 7560

The radio behind RC1 is an Atheros AR93xx whose EEPROM is blank, so it
enumerates as 168c:abcd -- AR9300_DEVID_INVALID -- and carries no
calibration data of its own. The data exists, in the board's urlader
partition, and an OpenWrt hotplug script extracts it into
ath9k-eeprom-pci-0001:01:00.0.bin. Getting ath9k to look for it there is
what this node is for.

ath9k_of_init() (drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/init.c) does nothing at
all unless the PCI device has a devicetree node: of_device_is_available()
on a NULL np is false and the function returns immediately. Only with a
node present, and only if that node carries "qca,no-eeprom", does it call
ath9k_eeprom_request() for the file above and clear AH_USE_EEPROM.

That flag is the whole mechanism. ath9k_hw_use_flash() is defined as its
negation, and ar9300_eeprom_restore_internal() consults it to decide
whether to take the ar9300_eeprom_restore_flash() path -- the one that
reads the requested firmware blob -- or to fall through to reading the
part's own EEPROM. Without the node the flag stays set, the fall-through
is taken, and there is nothing there to read. The radio does not come up
degraded; it does not come up.

Two nodes are needed because the calibration is a property of the
endpoint, not of the host bridge. The outer pcie@0 is the DesignWare root
port, devfn 0 on bus 0; wifi@0,0 inside it is the endpoint, devfn 0 on
bus 1. The PCI core matches devicetree nodes to devices by devfn alone,
walking down from the host bridge node, so the addresses are what
selects the device and the compatible string is documentation. The shape
is the one lantiq boards already use for the same part -- see
vr9_avm_fritz7430.dts in OpenWrt.

Nothing else about the port changes: the reset gpio, the swap policy and
the interrupt-map RC1 already has continue to apply. RC0 gets no
equivalent -- the 5 GHz radio there enumerates with its real id,
168c:003c, and is an ath10k part, whose calibration comes in by a
different route.

